course: dictionary
lesson: 3307
title: Words for good manners
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Etiquette means the rules of polite behaviour in a group or society. It tells you how to greet people, how to eat at the table and how to write a letter. «It is quite against animal-etiquette to dwell on possible trouble ahead» uses the word in this sense: there is a rule about what is polite to say.
explain: Look at the context to check the meaning. The sentence says etiquette «forbade» a comment, so the word must be about a rule. «Animal-etiquette forbade any sort of comment on the sudden disappearance of one's friends.» Forbade means did not allow, so etiquette is a set of rules that tells you what you may and may not say.
explain: Etiquette is a word for behaviour, not for one rule. Table etiquette is the rules for eating with others. Court etiquette is the rules for behaving at a royal court. A breach of etiquette is when someone breaks these rules, so you can read «a breach of etiquette» as «a rude thing to do».
fact: Etiquette means the rules of polite behaviour.
fact: Etiquette is uncountable, so say «good etiquette», not «a good etiquette».
fact: Phrases you may meet: table etiquette, court etiquette and a breach of etiquette.
fact: Online etiquette, sometimes called netiquette, means polite behaviour when you write and talk on the internet.
fact: «Animal-etiquette» in the example sentences is a playful, made-up word, and its meaning still comes from the word etiquette.
exercise: «It is quite against animal-etiquette to dwell on possible trouble ahead.» What does etiquette mean here? | *The rules of polite behaviour | A type of weather | A loud noise made by a crowd | A kind of food eaten by animals
exercise: Which of these is good table etiquette? | Reaching across the table for the biggest piece | *Waiting for your turn before you take food | Talking with your mouth full | Pushing in front of others at the table
exercise: Which of these is an example of online etiquette, or netiquette? | Sending the same message to everyone a hundred times | *Being kind and respectful in messages | Sharing other people's private photos without asking | Writing rude comments about strangers
test: «Animal-etiquette forbade any sort of comment on the sudden disappearance of one's friends.» What does forbade mean? | *Did not allow | Made fun of | Was scared of | Wanted very much
test: Which phrase means someone has broken the rules of polite behaviour? | Table etiquette | Court etiquette | *A breach of etiquette | Netiquette in the classroom
exam: Ella says etiquette means being rude on purpose to show you are brave. Is she right? | Yes, it is the same as a breach of etiquette | *No, etiquette means the rules of polite behaviour | Yes, it means being as loud as you can | No, it means only the rules for spelling
